1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
A king described a dying body so exactly it

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,620
reads like an eyewitness account of a crucifixion

3
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:06,380
he never saw.

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:08,600
Psalm twenty-two.

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,340
David wrote it in the first person, but he

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,900
was describing someone else's death, not his

7
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:15,200
own.

8
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,440
Watch the body of the dying man: "I am poured

9
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:23,680
out like water, and all my bones are out of

10
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:27,960
joint." Drained and every joint pulled loose,

11
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,200
the way a body hangs when it's suspended by

12
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:30,680
the arms.

13
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,900
And: "I may tell all my bones: they look and

14
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:37,240
stare upon me." He could count every bone,

15
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:40,140
stretched and exposed, while onlookers stood

16
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:41,000
and stared at him.

17
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:44,040
David never saw a crucifixion.

18
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,740
Rome would not make it its instrument for centuries.

19
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,300
Yet the dying man of his song bears the marks

20
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:51,820
of one written early.

21
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:56,420
This is what Jesus bore to bring you home every

22
00:00:56,420 --> 00:01:00,300
wrenched joint, every cold stare, recorded

23
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,180
centuries early so you'd know none of it was

24
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:03,480
